# Waiting is not a reaction trigger
# Give identical peroxide samples four different waits. Does elapsed time invent a reaction the model was never told to run?
# Intermediate mission. Inspect the complete inventory after 30, 60, 120 and
# 240 seconds: the clock advances, but an unregistered kinetic mechanism does
# not appear merely because time passed.
#
# Model boundary: this is a test of the bench contract, not a claim that real
# hydrogen peroxide is indefinitely stable. Real decomposition depends on
# concentration, temperature, light, impurities, surfaces and catalysts; none
# is inferred here. Use only teacher-prepared dilute peroxide, eye protection
# and clean compatible apparatus for any physical comparison. Do not ingest it
# or return used material to its stock container.
register lv2
add v1 water 200mL
add v1 H2O2 0.01mol
wait 30s
inspect v1
new
add v2 water 200mL
add v2 H2O2 0.01mol
wait 60s
inspect v2
new
add v3 water 200mL
add v3 H2O2 0.01mol
wait 120s
inspect v3
new
add v4 water 200mL
add v4 H2O2 0.01mol
wait 240s
inspect v4
